# Session management

Labwc can optionally integrate with the user session manager by writing
systemd user units under `~/.config/systemd/user/` when `<daemonize>enabled</daemonize>`
is set in `rc.xml`. On launch, labwc creates or refreshes:

- `labwc.service`
- `labwc-session.target`
- `labwc-shutdown.target`

When the setting is disabled, all three are removed again. None of the units
carry an `[Install]` section: they are started explicitly by the launcher
instead of being enabled persistently. An enablement symlink left behind by
older versions of this feature
(`~/.config/systemd/user/graphical-session.target.wants/labwc.service`) is
removed on both enable and disable.

A shipped launcher, `labwc-session`, handles first-run bootstrap and normal
systemd startup. Since the units are applied by labwc itself, the launcher
first runs `labwc --daemonize-apply`, which parses `rc.xml` and writes or
removes the units without starting the compositor. Switching
`<daemonize>enabled</daemonize>` in either direction therefore takes effect on
the very next login.

The launcher then starts `labwc.service` if the unit exists. If it is missing
(because `<daemonize>` is disabled, or on the very first run), it falls back to
`exec labwc` so the units are created on that run. When the compositor
terminates it starts `labwc-shutdown.target` to tear down
`graphical-session.target`, but only while that target is still active.

When labwc runs as a systemd user service it inherits the environment of the
user manager, which outlives individual sessions. If `WAYLAND_DISPLAY` or
`DISPLAY` points at a socket that no longer exists, labwc un-sets the variable
before selecting a wlroots backend, so the nested Wayland or X11 backend is not
picked up accidentally. `labwc-session` also clears those variables from the
manager environment before starting the service.

For users updating from an older install, stale user-local units can be
removed; labwc recreates them automatically once `<daemonize>enabled</daemonize>`
is configured. The launcher itself has to be installed for this to work, so
packages need to ship `data/labwc-session` next to the compositor binary.
